WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.450
- Guys, this is such exciting times.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.100
How did we get here?

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.100
- It was a hot summer day and
Ramin and Mathias' PhD advisor

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.530
professor Radu Grosu from

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.040
the Technical University of Vienna

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.160
was at a meeting in
Europe together with me

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.500
and we decided to go for a run together.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.980
And as we were running, we
were talking with each other

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.540
about the most exciting
ideas that were on our minds.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.990
And we started talking
about machine learning,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.860
about the large size of
machine learning models

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.900
and about how the brain

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.900
of the worm only has 300
neurons, 302 to be precise.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:49.860
And so we both thought that
this was just so exciting

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.760
to try to understand how
the natural brains work

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.310
and what we might be able to do.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.230
And at the same time, at MIT

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.360
we were working on autonomous driving

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.200
and we were developing these end-to-end

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.000
machine learning models that were

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.460
on the order of a
hundred thousand neurons.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.293
And I said,

00:01:09.293 --> 00:01:12.060
"Well, I wonder if we can
make our network smaller."

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.960
And so Rado said, "Hey, I
have two excellent students,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.450
Ramin Hasani and Mattia Lechner.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.760
Why don't we all get together
to see if we can do that?"

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.520
So they came and the rest is history,

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.770
Alexander because you joined the team

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:30.180
and we managed to change the model

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.060
for learning how to
drive by watching humans

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.960
from a hundred thousand
neurons to only 19 neurons.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.550
And that was just transformational.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.700
And to get it all done,
to get it implemented

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.590
and to get it actually
in action in the woods

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.120
was really extraordinary.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.310
- Yeah, and especially
those early networks when

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.470
we were testing them in Fort Devons

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:53.940
completely unseen environments,

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.430
training these models here in Cambridge

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.600
in urban environments

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.910
and then having these liquid
neural networks be deployed

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.120
in completely brand new unseen scenarios

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.552
was really revolutionary for the field.

00:02:04.552 --> 00:02:05.385
- [Man] Absolutely.

00:02:05.385 --> 00:02:08.550
- It was really extraordinary to see how

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:12.510
we could train a model
by driving in the city

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.720
and have that model perform accurately

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.700
and correctly without further training

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.970
in a completely different
environment in the woods.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.390
And we started wondering,
why is this happening?

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:28.080
And that's because of the different nature

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.230
of liquid networks and
which this was the focus

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.230
of Ramin's thesis and Mathias' thesis.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:38.220
- So we focused on the brain
of the worm, as you mentioned.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.680
We started understanding
how neurons communicate

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.410
with each other in order
to create behavior.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:48.150
And we wanted to create kind of, you know

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.630
a level of abstraction of
models that is close enough

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.660
to the natural learning systems.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.750
But at the same time they are executable,

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:58.950
and you can actually run them

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.560
on real world applications.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.570
And then, so we came up

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.510
with this abstract model that each node

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:10.204
of the system is given by
a differential equation.

00:03:10.204 --> 00:03:13.530
And they are communicating with each other

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.010
through synapses that
are nonlinear functions,

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.320
that are input dependent.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.370
And this way you can
create a learning system

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.500
that is much more abstract

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.860
and small at the same
time is more expressive.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.900
And throughout the years,
like we showed a lot

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.000
of theoretical properties of these models,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.160
we showed that they're
most expressive type

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.470
of continuous time neural networks.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.560
We showed them that they can

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.560
model time series data really well.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.950
And obviously they become
like a perfect candidate

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.280
for closed loop and kind of
safety critical applications,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.670
like autonomous driving or
autonomous navigations of drones

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.740
or medical time series modeling

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:00.060
and those kind of applications.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.577
And the core behind these things.

00:04:02.577 --> 00:04:06.300
Like we were also like wondering
like how is that possible

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.990
that we can get such good
results for driving into

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.540
like performing lane keeping very robust

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.390
while having 19 neurons.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:17.490
Then we started studying
and then we figured out

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:21.630
that the true nature of these
equations coming together

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.090
is the fact that they can
capture the causal structure

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.480
of a task so they can capture causality.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:32.480
That means they can learn
proper better representations

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:33.630
from data.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:37.530
So we showed a lot of properties,
theoretical properties

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.760
of the systems such as
universality, such as expressivity

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.430
and robustness characteristics.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.830
And then we scaled them

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:49.773
into applications in autonomous driving.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.480
- With liquid neural networks,

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.640
we're able to achieve
this incredible property

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.550
which is the ability to train
in one type of environment

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:02.280
but then deploy in a brand
new unseen environment

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.560
that the car has never driven in before.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.380
Similar to how we as humans
are able to learn how to drive

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.720
in one city and then be
placed in a brand new city

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.140
and also be able to successfully
navigate those new roads.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:17.730
- Mathias and Alexander, what
did it take for you to go

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:20.460
from the theoretical
models to the implemented

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:23.160
systems that have such
extraordinary behavior?

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.212
It was so exciting to see the results.

00:05:25.212 --> 00:05:27.990
- Yeah, it's actually was
a very challenging task

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.060
because these models were typically used

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.520
in neuroscience research
and we try to bring it

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.710
into like a machine learning framework

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:38.070
and as well as being able
to run on embedded systems

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.510
right on our robot,

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:41.710
where you have some real time requirements

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.610
so you need to be really fast.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.610
Moreover, the structure of
typical machine learning models

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:49.620
is fully connected so you can write it

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.240
as a matrix multiplication.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.790
However, like because our
models are really sparse,

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.830
so we had to come up with clever ideas

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.700
of how to make use of this
kind of wire structure

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.910
and be very efficient
in terms of computation.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:06.210
- How did you collaborate
with Alexander on that?

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.820
- Yeah, Alexander shared
like some really nice codes

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:13.200
that is able to train a
model that runs on a car.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.730
And basically we modified
it by combining like

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:20.130
the work that I did during (indistinct)

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:22.110
with Ramin and Rado

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.783
and combined it with Alexander's code such

00:06:24.783 --> 00:06:28.560
that we have a model that can
be trained to drive the car.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.100
- Alexander the C cell
robot car is your baby,

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.753
how did you trust his code?

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:39.150
- So these models are built off
of the brain wiring of worm.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.598
So we drove the car very slowly at first.

00:06:41.598 --> 00:06:42.681
(group laughing)

00:06:42.681 --> 00:06:44.283
Speed of a worm.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.590
- And eventually what
was the maximum speed?

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:48.423
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:48.423 --> 00:06:50.640
Eventually we pushed
the car to its limits,

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.473
it steering limits.

00:06:51.473 --> 00:06:53.010
In Fort Devons, really the maximum

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:56.460
that we could steer these
cars was 30, 35 miles an hour.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.140
It's a very close track.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.360
So even that speed is even difficult

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:01.740
for humans to drive at that speed.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.530
So we were able to really push
these models to their limits,

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:05.850
their physical limits on the car

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.850
which was a huge win
and huge success for us.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:12.480
- And I think that we took it
even further when we took it

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.790
to the drone environment and flying.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:19.320
So we went from the 2D kind
of camera to control actions

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:23.017
or navigation signals
into the flying drones

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.940
and towards objectives.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.970
And not only that, but I mean navigation

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:33.420
of flying agents has
been there for decades.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:35.580
But what's what's interesting is

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.824
that how can we have
intelligent systems that they

00:07:38.824 --> 00:07:42.300
can extract the task that they're given.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:44.250
So, and this is a big question,

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.170
fundamental question in machine learning

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.660
that how can we extract the actual task

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.900
from given data to a
machine learning system?

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:52.770
And I think this is one

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.170
of those early evidences that we show

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.360
that their liquid neural networks

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.560
they can perform reasoning

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.463
so they can understand the
task that is given to them.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:08.370
- Quite similar to the
end-to-end driving scenario

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:09.960
we thought we would take this

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:12.390
to a larger dimensional space where now

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:13.890
we can take it to flight

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:18.690
and use the liquid networks to
pilot the drone autonomously

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:21.330
for a set task that we set out for it.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.070
In this case, the task we trained it

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:26.070
on is to fly towards a series
target that we show it.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.620
A human pilot manually
flies the drone a few times

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.660
towards some targets that are set out

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:32.160
that we trained in some woods.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:33.600
And then we take this data

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.730
and train the liquid
networks on these sequences

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:38.910
of data and then we can take the networks

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:41.490
and run them in a variety
of different environments

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.230
on tasks that are exactly the same

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.870
but also slightly different, but related.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:49.320
What we're looking to do
next is add multimodality

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:50.153
to these networks.

00:08:50.153 --> 00:08:52.290
So at the moment they
only take input images

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:53.790
to run the networks.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:55.500
We want to see what else we can do,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.570
what other information can
we feed into the network

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:00.360
to have it achieve more complex behavior.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:02.640
And as we do that, we'll
also be looking to scale it

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.920
to multi-agent systems
where we can get not only

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:06.180
one drone achieving a task,

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:07.710
but multiple drones communicating

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.750
and achieving more complex tasks.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.310
- There are so many ways

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:16.310
in which the function of
these networks surprised us

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.320
in an exciting and positive way.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:20.430
First of all, I wanna say

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:23.970
that I enjoyed so much going to the track

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:28.230
and doing the experiments
and I had full faith.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:32.880
In fact, I put my car, my own
personal beloved car was one

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.350
of the obstacles that our
vehicle was trying to avoid.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:41.430
And well there were no
accidents, very happy about that.

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:44.160
But going back to what
you were saying, Ramin,

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:49.160
the causality property of
these networks is very powerful

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:54.380
and it's really amazing
to see how experimentally

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.510
we can achieve zero shot learning

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:59.520
on fairly complicated tasks.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.090
And the tasks we studied
most recently is the task

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.270
of identifying an object
and tracking an object.

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:07.620
And we could do this

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.250
without actually explicitly
telling the system

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:16.200
your task is to find this red
backpack and follow it around.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:21.200
And so we developed the
solution and then we trained

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.500
in the woods using imaging from the summer

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.000
when the scene is very
(indistinct), it's green,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.390
it's really a summer scene.

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:35.370
And then the surprising thing was

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:37.740
that we could take the same code

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:42.360
without any further training
and we could show it working

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:44.970
in the fall when we still have leaves

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:48.480
but of a very different texture and color.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.000
And most extraordinarily
we were able to do

00:10:53.441 --> 00:10:55.560
the same model and the same solution

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.660
for winter where all the leaves were gone

00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:02.660
and all we had was black vertical
lines for the tree trunks.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.740
And when we compared our
solution with the solution

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:11.430
of other traditional models
that were trained exactly

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:13.530
as we trained our solution,

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:16.440
we found that ours was
the only one that worked.

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:18.360
In other words liquid networks

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:22.290
were the only ones that could
go from the task trained

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:25.350
in the summer to executing
the task in the fall

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.600
and executing the task in the winter.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.990
And I said the winter experiments
were most extraordinary

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:34.170
but actually we had even more
extraordinary experiments

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:37.770
when we went from the woods
into urban environments

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.620
and from static objects to moving objects.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:44.160
And the same models operated
extraordinarily well.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.020
And the way we measured that is by looking

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:50.190
at the attention of the networks
and by showing that in fact

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:54.840
with liquid networks, the
attention is very much focused

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:58.560
on the task, whereas with other solutions

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:01.410
the attention is often
focused on the context.

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:03.660
And so that's why with other solutions,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:08.520
when you change the context,
you need to change the model.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:10.710
But with us, with our solution,

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:13.770
with liquid networks, it's very different.

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:15.120
- Yeah, to analyze network

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.910
and to really understand like
what has been learned, right?

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:19.950
Because these networks
behave as a black box.

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:22.650
We use a technique called resiliency maps,

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:25.050
where we visualize where
the network is looking at,

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:27.300
where the network is strong attention to

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:30.266
and really to understand
actually what's yeah,

00:12:30.266 --> 00:12:31.890
what the network is doing.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:35.378
And we have been using a couple
of methods maybe, Alexander.

00:12:35.378 --> 00:12:38.400
- Yeah, so resiliency
maps basically allow us

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.850
to ask this question to
our models, which is,

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.220
if you make some decision, why
did you make this decision?

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:45.600
What did you see in your input

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:49.050
and your environment that
made you make this decision?

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:50.610
So resiliency maps are a very visual

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:53.580
and explanatory way for us to
ask those types of questions

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:56.370
to these very traditionally
black box models.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:59.130
But when we ask these to
liquid neural networks

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:00.540
we're able to get very crisp

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:04.080
and concise answers in the
attention maps that we see back.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:07.350
And the attention is always
very focused like Daniella said,

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:09.510
exactly on the task that's
supposed to be performing,

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:12.840
even though we never give it
any explicit instruction to

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.790
pay attention in those areas.

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:18.360
- So now this was a very
beautiful experimental result

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:20.610
that encouraged us to think

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.790
about deeper questions
around machine learning.

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:25.500
And one of the grand challenges

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.020
of machine learning is how can we get

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.690
a learning based solution
that is interpretable

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:31.920
and explainable?

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.140
And this is especially important

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:36.330
for safety critical applications,

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:39.033
like the ones we encounter in robotics.

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:43.830
With liquid networks, because
the solutions we have,

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:46.170
because the models are compact,

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.710
for example, in the
case of the driving task

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:51.810
we only need 19 neurons.

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:54.330
Because we have such compact solutions

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:57.420
we can actually associate the behavior

00:13:57.420 --> 00:14:00.270
of the network with a decision tree,

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:04.050
which is computable and
it is human understandable

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:09.050
and produces in a more theoretical
framework an explanation

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:12.240
for how these models
reach their decisions.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.910
And I think this is an important next step

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:19.260
towards developing solutions
that are interpretable

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:23.220
and even certifiable for
safety critical applications.

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:26.970
- I think the of artificial intelligence

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:28.080
now very much focused

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:30.630
on the generative AI aspect of things.

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:33.450
And in this space we see that larger

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:36.030
and larger models are getting,

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:39.300
they're booming and they're
performing exceptionally well.

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:40.500
They're getting better and better.

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:43.750
But I think what we have with
liquid networks is kind of

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:50.400
a counter to that kind
of scaling law that shows

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:54.270
that you can actually
achieve intelligent behavior

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.280
and especially in the task

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:58.770
of drone manipulation that
we were talking about,

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:01.410
we were training in a complete forest

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:05.250
and then we kind of zero
shot completely transferred

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.290
it into a urban area.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:09.107
That's fascinating.

00:15:09.107 --> 00:15:12.750
That's complete out of
distribution generalization.

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:14.100
This behavior emerging

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:18.570
from a network that has 30 liquid neurons

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:22.350
that's I think something that is requires

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:23.340
the attention of the field.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:27.210
So the field has to actually look into how

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:29.550
heavily under parametrized
neural networks,

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:33.060
such as liquid neural networks,
because of their structure

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:35.970
they can actually generate behavior

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:38.340
and become kind of a potential

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:42.840
for the future generation
of our generative models.

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:44.130
- So that's one direction,

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:46.890
but there are many
other directions, right?

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:51.420
So we would like to have
deeper theoretical results

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:56.420
in understanding the causality,
in defining the mathematics

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:59.190
behind the attention maps,

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:04.190
in expanding more on our
interpretability research

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:07.740
to really get to the
point of certifiability.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:08.820
I mean, can you imagine

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:10.950
if we get to the point where we have

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:14.610
machine learning based
solutions that are certifiable,

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:16.260
that would be extraordinary.

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:19.950
Because we would be ready
to deploy the solutions

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:24.259
in industry where not making
mistakes is actually important.

00:16:24.259 --> 00:16:25.092
- Absolutely.

00:16:25.092 --> 00:16:27.240
- And so that's very, very exciting.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:31.680
The other area that I think
is extraordinarily exciting

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:36.180
is the area of considering
multiple agents,

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:37.380
so multiple robots

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:41.880
or multiple computational units
interacting with each other.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:43.200
And I'm very excited

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:47.490
about developing a robust and
rigorous theoretical solution

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:50.790
for how we compose solutions that work

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:55.470
on one application into solutions.

00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:58.560
And there's just so
much more, for instance,

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:01.860
in medicine, in providing surgeons

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:04.440
with powerful solutions to prevent them

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:08.430
from making mistakes is an area
where we're actively working

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:10.860
with Mathias right now.

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:14.250
And another very important
area is to really think

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:16.020
about the scope of these solutions.

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:21.020
So right now all of our results
are for time series data.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:25.680
That means that applications
that involve video

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:28.500
or involve sensor output,

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:31.980
these applications are
amenable to liquid networks.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:35.040
But how would these networks perform

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:36.600
on other types of data?

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:40.320
Can we extend these
extraordinary applications

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:44.760
and performance to
static data, like images

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:49.760
or to text, to a completely
new kind of data source?

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:53.130
- I think liquid networks
can serve as a building block

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:56.400
of the future artificial
intelligence systems

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:58.710
and maybe artificial general
intelligence systems.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:17:59.610
And that's my dream

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:02.820
and that's what I'm
gonna work on the future.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:05.490
- I wanna build generalist
liquid neural networks

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:07.440
that don't solve just a single task,

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:09.420
but a wide variety of tasks

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:12.510
and perform really well
across the board of tasks.

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:16.200
- I really believe, and my
dream is for AI to be largely

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.930
embedded into society,
helping us every single day

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:20.220
with a lot of our tasks.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:22.470
And a lot of the really exciting
advances that we've seen

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:26.340
with AI to date have all been
non embodied intelligence.

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:28.020
So one of the things
that really excites me is

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:29.970
the way that liquid neural networks,

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:32.070
we've now shown this
capability where we can

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:34.620
deploy intelligence into embodied agents

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:37.860
and have intelligence be
actually embedded into society

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:41.400
and helping all of us as
humans in our everyday lives.

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:46.400
- With liquid models, we
begin to understand rigorously

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:50.640
and more theoretically how learning works.

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:54.270
And I'm really excited about
pushing the boundaries here.

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:57.990
Understanding intelligence is
one of the greatest challenges

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:01.020
in science today, and I'm so excited

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:04.290
about the role that
liquid networks will play

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:06.630
in answering many questions

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:09.300
whose answers we do not know today.

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:11.010
With Liquid Networks

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:14.100
we can also create
machine learning solutions

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:17.070
that are explainable,
compact, sustainable,

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.200
perhaps even certifiable.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:22.290
And I'm so excited about the next steps

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:25.530
in developing the mathematical foundations

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:29.640
of liquid networks and
understanding the foundations

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:33.210
for a future with machine
learning that is sustainable,

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:36.303
explainable, causal, and composable.

